1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,489
Early May in Bad Salzungen, Thuringia. Your vantage is a neighbouring chimney, two meters from a white stork eyrie.

2
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,144
Look down. The town is still asleep. Up here, the day has already started.

3
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:55,003
Weighing up to several hundred kilograms over decades of reuse, this woven platform shelters three week-old nestlings.

4
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,337
Listen behind you and overhead. The foraging partner returns from the Werra floodplain on broad slotted wings.

5
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,078
Watch their greeting straight ahead. Adult white storks have a reduced syrinx and are nearly voiceless.

6
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,928
Instead, they arch their necks backward and amplify rapid mechanical bill-clattering through an inflated throat pouch.

7
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:32,172
The returning parent regurgitates earthworms, amphibians, and meadow invertebrates directly onto the nest floor.

8
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:53,773
Unlike raptors that tear prey bill-to-bill, storks provision onto the cup so chicks feed independently from hatching.

9
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,480
Field growth curves show hatchlings emerge around seventy grams and double their body mass in roughly five days.

10
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,138
Within forty-five days, that steep logistic climb carries each nestling past three point four kilograms.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,954
Straight ahead, the eldest nestling rises into the breeze to test its developing black primary remiges.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,485
Repeated bouts of tethered wing-flapping condition the pectoralis muscles weeks before fledging at two months old.

13
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,209
Look down across the gables to your left. Early commuters and a waking town bakery mark seven in the morning.

14
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,928
Human rooftops replaced ancient floodplain trees across Central Europe, turning towns like this into synanthropic breeding strongholds.

15
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:32,433
This nest has its own live camera — Stork nest, Bad Salzungen, on Digital Live Zoo. Go and see who is home right now.
